---
import StandardPageLayout from "../../../layouts/StandardPageLayout.astro";
import PageToc from "../../../components/PageToc.astro";
import "../../../styles/standards-poster.css";
import "../../../styles/extended.css";
import metadata from "../../../data/standards/hgb/drs27.json";
---

<StandardPageLayout
  code={metadata.code}
  standardType={metadata.framework}
  title={metadata.title}
  subtitle={metadata.nativeTitle}
  framework="hgb"
  status={metadata.status}
>
  <Fragment slot="toc">
    <PageToc
      items={[
        { id: "s1", label: "Scope" },        { id: "s2", label: "The election framework" },        { id: "s3", label: "Worked example" },        { id: "s4", label: "IFRS" }
      ]}
    />
  </Fragment>

  <div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Scope — Elaborating §310's Genuine Election</h2><span class="line"></span></div><div class="grid">
<div class="card info span2">
<div class="ctitle">The second of the three consolidation techniques, now fully documented</div>
<div class="principle">DRS 27 elaborates §310 HGB's own proportionate consolidation election for jointly-managed enterprises — established in the earlier HGB poster series as one of THREE major consolidation techniques (alongside full consolidation and the equity method), and genuinely notable for being an ELECTION rather than a mandate, unlike full consolidation's own compulsory application wherever control exists.</div>
</div>
</div><div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>The Election Framework, Elaborated</h2><span class="line"></span></div><div class="grid">
<div class="card warn span2">
<div class="ctitle">Genuine guidance on the choice between proportionate consolidation and the equity method</div>
<div class="principle">Recall from the earlier HGB poster: a qualifying jointly-managed enterprise presents a genuine fork — proportionate consolidation under §310, or the equity method under §311/§312 (elaborated by DRS 26). DRS 27 provides detailed guidance on APPLYING the borrowed full-consolidation toolkit (§310(2)'s own "mit den erforderlichen Abweichungen" cross-reference) proportionately — capital consolidation, debt elimination, and profit elimination, all scaled to the joint venturer's own percentage share rather than the full amount.</div>
</div>
</div><div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>Worked Example — Debt Elimination, Applied Proportionately</h2><span class="line"></span></div><div class="grid">
<div class="card span2">
<div class="worked">
<div class="wk-title">Eliminating intragroup debt at only the proportionate share</div>
<div class="wk-body">Recall the 50/50 joint venture example from the earlier HGB poster. The joint venture owes the parent €600,000 on an intragroup loan. The parent has elected proportionate consolidation, bringing in 50% of the joint venture's own line items.</div>
<div class="wk-answer">Under DRS 27, the parent eliminates only its OWN 50% share of this intragroup debt — €300,000 — against the CORRESPONDING 50% of the joint venture's own receivable/payable already brought into the group accounts, rather than attempting to eliminate the FULL €600,000, which would improperly eliminate an amount exceeding what was actually consolidated in the first place.</div>
</div>
</div>
</div><div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>Relationship to IFRS (IFRS 11)</h2><span class="line"></span></div><div class="grid">
<div class="card suc span2">
<div class="ctitle">Continuing the cross-library divergence flagged at the very start of this topic</div>
<div class="principle">Recall the genuinely notable three-way comparison this poster series flagged when originally covering §310: both HGB (via DRS 27) and ASPE (Canadian private-company GAAP) still offer proportionate consolidation as a genuine election for joint ventures, while IFRS 11 eliminated the equivalent option for genuine joint ventures in 2011, mandating the equity method instead — reserving proportionate-style treatment only for the narrower "joint operations" category. DRS 27's continued elaboration of a genuine, live election confirms this divergence remains fully operative in current German practice, even as IFRS moved decisively away from it over a decade ago.</div>
</div>
</div>
</StandardPageLayout>
